+## 2026-08-16 — In-game legibility: invisible chokepoints are defects (user ruling 2026-08-14, applied)
+
+*(Provenance: the user's 2026-08-14 ruling — "if players need a manual to play, that's a design failure" — as ratified by the v2-visibility job; the surge-explainer's Section C named the five gaps, each confirmed in code.)*
+
+- **Origin:** the surge-explainer (`implementation-artifacts/surge-explainer/surge-explainer.html`) diagnosed five invisible design gaps — the E22 pool cap + map-wide shedding had no HUD surface, drop sites were never rendered, the 6-packet lane bound had no depth readout, the SLA gauges never said *why* a packet dropped, and the strain rings didn't say *what* was stuck. The user's own confusion in the surge run (packets vanishing with no visible cause; unexplained rings) is **evidence of real visibility debt, not user error** — the ruling that made this job.
+- **Decision (canon):** legibility in-game is a **design requirement**. Invisible chokepoints are **defects, not mysteries**; every loss state the player can hit must be **readable without a manual** — telegraph in-game, never docs out-of-game. The HUD must stay quiet when healthy (the 5.2 quiet-at-🟢 canon applies to every surface); the surfaces must read the sim's own derived state (never re-record, never a second serialization).
+- **Applied (this job):** five presentation-side surfaces, all app-layer (drawn from `draw_hud` — the harness T2 capture path never calls it, so goldens cannot shift; no `LOG_VERSION` bump, T1 byte-identical):
+  1. **Pool-pressure gauge** (gap 1) — the E22 pool cap + map-wide shedding now have a HUD surface: in-flight count vs `pool_max_packets`, a fill bar colored by the shared `warnings` thresholds, and a SHEDDING tag when E22 drops land.
+  2. **Drop-site markers** (gap 2) — a fading marker at the shed bundle's midpoint (E9 sites), drawn from the per-tick `Drop_Site` scratch; E22 sites are map-wide by construction and ride the pool gauge's tag.
+  3. **Queue-depth readout** (gap 3) — the 5.8 QoS panel's last row shows the selected pipe's bundle queue depth per lane vs the E9 bound (`lane_queue_packets`).
+  4. **Why-dropped reason split** (gap 4) — the SLA gauges now carry "pool N / queue M" per class, fed from the drop-reason tag that already existed in every drop event.
+  5. **Node-strain cause** (gap 5) — the 5.2 node-health card gains a stuck-by-class line (which class, how many packets) when a node is actually stuck.
+- **What this does NOT change:** no balance numbers, no mechanics, no new command kinds, no serialization surface. The pool cap stays fixed (it is a protection, not a lever — the explainer's Section B); the traffic-model threads (5.9–5.12) remain out of scope on their own slots. Terminology: new HUD strings use the renamed networking terms (drop precedence, congestion, lane stays lane on the player HUD) per the terminology audit (merged 2026-08-16, PR #55 — this job rebased onto it).
